    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "71ej987",
      "content": "Physics is the branch of science that deals with matter, energy, motion, force, heat, light and sound."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "5jg5anq",
      "content": "It explains the natural phenomena occurring around us using scientific laws."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "gl6ie3n",
      "content": "Examples: "
    },
    {
      "id": "1h7rgeg",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<ul><li>Motion of vehicles – Mechanics</li><li>Flow of electricity – Electricity</li><li>Boiling of water – Heat</li><li>Rainbow formation – Optics</li></ul>",
        "Motion of vehicles – Mechanics",
        "Flow of electricity – Electricity",
        "Boiling of water – Heat",
        "Rainbow formation – Optics"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "54h8xgl",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "PHYSICAL QUANTITY"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Fundamental quantities (Mass, Length, Time, Temperature, Electric Current, Luminous Intensity, Amount of Substance) are independent of other physical quantities.",
        "Derived quantities such as velocity, acceleration, force, and density are expressed in terms of fundamental quantities.",
        "The SI system consists of 7 base units and 2 supplementary units (Radian for plane angle and Steradian for solid angle).",
        "Dimensional formulas represent physical quantities in terms of basic dimensions [M], [L], and [T].",
        "Density is defined as mass per unit volume and has the SI unit kg/m³."
      ],
      "id": "ykhs9wv"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "5ldrdft",
      "content": "A physical quantity is any quantity that can be measured and expressed in numerical value with a unit."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "uobgdso",
      "content": "Example:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "zmfkd9f",
      "content": "Length = 10 m Mass = 5 kg Time = 20 s"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "r0uwmvt",
      "content": "Without unit, a number has no physical meaning."
    },
    {
      "id": "4014rdt",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "TYPES OF PHYSICAL QUANTITIES"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "e523ul7",
      "content": "Physical quantities are of two types:"
    },
    {
      "id": "x369os9",
      "type": "numberedList",
      "items": [
        "Fundamental Quantities",
        "Derived Quantities"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "42porqg",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "FUNDAMENTAL QUANTITIES"
    },
    {
      "id": "syk8ulg",
      "type": "image",
      "imageId": "437c8cy",
      "src": "/books/Guide/GENERAL KNOWLEDGE/images/image_437c8cy.png"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "cfaokan",
      "content": "Fundamental quantities are basic quantities which cannot be derived from other quantities.<img></img>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "1yn9q16",
      "content": "There are seven fundamental quantities in SI system."
    },
    {
      "id": "xicxil3",
      "type": "numberedList",
      "items": [
        "Length – Meter (m)",
        "Mass – Kilogram (kg)",
        "Time – Second (s)",
        "Temperature – Kelvin (K)",
        "Electric Current – Ampere (A)",
        "Luminous Intensity – Candela (cd)",
        "Amount of Substance – Mole (mol)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "hcaautq",
      "content": "Trick to remember:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "icwa2ii",
      "content": "\"My Teacher Likes Playing Chess Daily Morning\""
    },
    {
      "id": "gbyurdv",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "DERIVED QUANTITIES"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "heiob6p",
      "content": "Derived quantities are obtained from fundamental quantities. Examples:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "cow3bga",
      "content": "Speed = Distance / Time"
    },
    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "Physical quantities are those quantities which can be measured directly or indirectly and in terms of which the laws of physics can be expressed.",
      "id": "piw0mqa"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "toggdt2",
      "content": "Acceleration = Change in velocity / Time Force = Mass × Acceleration"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "3bb0bek",
      "content": "Work = Force × Distance Power = Work / Time Density = Mass / Volume"
    },
    {
      "id": "f3lkxk8",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "SI SYSTEM OF UNITS"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "2yzo2o4",
      "content": "SI stands for International System of Units."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "elz697a",
      "content": "It is accepted globally and used in scientific measurements. It was adopted in the year 1960."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "58ymogk",
      "content": "Important SI Units: Length – Meter Mass – Kilogram Time – Second Force – Newton Energy – Joule"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "715zqya",
      "content": "Pressure – Pascal"
    },
    {
      "id": "6kkb8kb",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "CONVERSION OF UNITS"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "ewbin8u",
      "content": "1 kilometer = 1000 meters"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "w8swaz2",
      "content": "1 meter = 100 centimeters"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "qfg4egd",
      "content": "1 kilogram = 1000 grams"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "5cwmo5l",
      "content": "1 hour = 3600 seconds"
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Feature",
        "Fundamental Quantities",
